  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe cooler airmass fully settles in by Thursday as a Canadian \nsurface high, centered near Winnipeg, advects a cold and dry arctic \nairmass into the Upper Midwest. This continued cold air advection \nwill limit Thursday's high temperatures into the mid-40s, a chilly \ntrend that will hold through the end of the workweek. With the \nCanadian high firmly in control, skies will remain largely clear \nThursday night into Friday morning. The combination of clear skies, \nlight winds and a very dry boundary layer will set the stage for \noptimal radiational cooling, allowing for overnight lows Thursday \nand Friday nights to plummet into the lower to mid 20s. Dry and\nquiet conditions persist through Saturday.\n\nOur next potential window for precipitation arrives Sunday morning \nas a mid-level low tracks across the Southern Plains. Moisture \nreturn ahead of this system could spark a few showers across the \nPlains; however, there remains considerable uncertainty regarding \nthe system's northward extent. Depending on the exact track, some \nlight showers could skirt southwest Iowa. That being said, \npersistent dry air in the low levels may prove difficult to \novercome, likely limiting measurable precipitation this far north. \nGiven the uncertainty, low-end 15-20% PoPs have been introduced for \nsouthwest Iowa, and trends will be closely monitored for future \nadjustments. Subsidence and dry conditions are expected to return by \nthe start of next week, accompanied by a slight moderation in the \nairmass as highs rebound back to the 50s.",
